Define the float-to-int conversions UBSan flags in xsf.

Converting a double that is NaN or outside int's range to int is undefined
behaviour in C++; clang's UBSan reports it, and so does CRAN's UBSan check.
These guards keep what the code did by accident on x86 (the conversion gave
INT_MIN, which failed the comparison or matched no case):

- cephes/beta.h: test the range before `x == static_cast<int>(x)`, so a
  whole number too large for int fails the negative-integer test as it
  did. Reached by beta(-1, 1e10), lbeta(-1, 1e200), beta(0.5, -8.98e307).
- faddeeva.h: a NaN y100 switches on -1, falling through to the code after
  the switch as the comment there intends. Reached by erfcx(NaN),
  dawsn(NaN), erfi(NaN), wofz(NaN + 0i).

Upstream: https://github.com/scipy/xsf/issues/338

